ClearChat sync can stay and call `void createConversation()` internally; but before creating, reset. Then `conversationId` from response. Good.

Memory refresh:
- After `memory` SSE event, append observation to state (avoid duplicates by id).
- `refreshMemory` fetch.
- `updateObservation` calls API then updates state.
- `addObservation` for manual add.
- `clearMemory` calls API then clears observations and maybe resets chat? The API clears conversations too, so clear messages and conversation. Could be in Settings. Add.

Need `memoryLoaded`? Not essential. But the panel may show "loading" initially. Add `memoryLoading`.

Boot order:
```ts
async function boot() {
  // existing config
  ...
  const [latest, observations] = await Promise.all([
    fetchLatestConversation().catch(() => null),
    fetchMemory().catch(() => []),
  ]);
  if (!cancelled) {
    setObservations(observations);
    if (latest && latest.turns.length > 0) {
      setConversationId(latest.id);
      setMessages(storedConversationToMessages(latest));
    } else if (latest) {
      setConversationId(latest.id);
    }
  }
}
```
If no conversation, `conversationId` null; first `sendMessage` sends null and backend creates one, emits `conversation`. Good. But if we send `conversation_id: null`, Pydantic accepts. Fine.
When `conversation` event arrives: set `conversationId`.

Potential issue: `welcomeMessage` is included in `messages` and sent as history to the backend. Currently the backend receives the welcome assistant message too. That's okay. But persisted user turn only. The backend's memory context plus history. Fine.
However, when resuming, the welcome message should not be added if there are turns. Good.
When the user sends the first message, `messages` includes welcome + user; history includes welcome. Fine.
When the backend emits `conversation`, the user turn was already persisted by backend. Good.

`storedConversationToMessages`:
```ts
function storedPartsToChatParts(parts: StoredChatPart[]): ChatPart[] {
  const out: ChatPart[] = [];
  for (const part of parts) {
    if (part.kind === "text") {
      if (part.text) out.push({kind:"text", text:part.text});
    } else if (part.kind === "ui") {
      const parsed = parseUiSpec(part.spec, CATALOG_MANIFEST);
      if (parsed.ok) out.push({kind:"ui", id:part.id, node:parsed.node});
    }
  }
  return out;
}
```
If stored assistant content has text but parts empty (old data), fall back to content text. For user turns, parts likely text.

```ts
function storedConversationToMessages(conversation: StoredConversation): ChatMessage[] {
  return conversation.turns.map(turn => {
    const parts = storedPartsToChatParts(turn.parts);
    if (parts.length === 0 && turn.content) {
      parts.push({kind:"text", text:turn.content});
    }
    return { role: turn.role, content: turn.content || parts.filter(p=>p.kind==="text").map(...).join(""), parts };
  }).filter(m => m.parts.length > 0);
}
```
Role type matches.

Observation SSE:
```ts
case "memory": {
  const observation = event.data.observation as Observation | undefined;
  if (observation?.id) setObservations(prev => prev.some(o=>o.id===observation.id) ? prev.map(...) : [observation, ...prev]);
  break;
}
case "conversation":
  setConversationId(String(event.data.id ?? ""));
  break;
```
Also `memory` event after remember.

`sendMessage` request includes `conversation_id: conversationId` (state). But we may not have it yet; backend emits. Use `conversationIdRef` to avoid stale? `conversationId` state closure within sendMessage is fine; after first event, next send uses updated state. But if user sends again before state update? streaming prevents. Fine.

`clearChat`:
```ts
const clearChat = useCallback(() => {
  abortRef.current?.abort();
  setMessages([welcomeMessage()]);
  ...
  void createConversation().then(c => setConversationId(c.id)).catch(...)
}, [pushToast]);
```
But `createConversation` API returns object. If it fails, leave null. Also maybe `setToolActivity`.
Should clearing chat clear memory? No; user can clear memory separately. Settings has "Clear conversation" perhaps.

`refreshMemory` callback.
`updateObservation` callback:
```ts
const updateObservation = useCallback(async (id, patch) => {
  try {
    const observation = await apiUpdateObservation(id, patch);
    setObservations(prev => prev.map(o => o.id === id ? observation : o));
  } catch (error) { pushToast("error", ...) }
}, [pushToast]);
```
`addObservation`:
```ts
const addObservation = useCallback(async (payload) => { ... }, [pushToast]);
```
`clearMemory`:
```ts
const clearMemory = useCallback(async () => {
  try { await apiClearMemory(); setObservations([]); setMessages([welcomeMessage()]); setConversationId(null); ... } catch...
}, [pushToast]);
```
But API `DELETE /api/memory` clears conversations, so yes reset chat. Maybe create a new conversation after clearing? If `conversationId` null, next message creates. Good.
Interface names can be `saveObservation`, `removeObservation`? We'll use `updateObservation`, `addObservation`, `clearMemory`, `refreshMemory`, `memory`, `memoryLoading`.

